{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_sclmdnm4jxvuoat3kjsf54uoquqffll2gxkzb52fun6qsq7midaq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01YZvbkHLv2Eg5P9itESH8Wf","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-dbP4qF/translation/SYMBOLS.md","old_string":"The C `.so` imports `printf` from libc. The Rust `.so` imports the same\n`printf` (declared via `extern \"C\" { fn printf(...) }`) rather than using\nRust's `std::io`, so stdout formatting *and* stream buffering are the identical\nlibc code path. 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols in the Rust `.so`.\n\n```\n$ nm -D --undefined-only translation/target/release/libdriver.so | grep -v GLIBC_ | grep -v 'GCC_'\n(only libc/Rust-runtime imports; no unresolved project symbols)\n```","new_string":"The C `.so` imports exactly:\n\n```\nprintf@GLIBC_2.2.5   U\nputchar@GLIBC_2.2.5  U     <- GCC rewrites printf(\"\\n\") into putchar('\\n')\n_ITM_*/__cxa_finalize/__gmon_start__   w  (weak CRT hooks)\n```\n\nThe Rust `.so` imports the same `printf` **and** the same `putchar` (LLVM\napplies the identical `printf(\"\\n\")` → `putchar('\\n')` simplification), because\nthe translation calls libc `printf` via `extern \"C\"` rather than using Rust's\n`std::io`. Stdout formatting *and* stream buffering therefore go through the\nidentical libc code path in both libraries. The rest of the Rust `.so`'s\nundefined list is std/`libunwind`/libc (`malloc`, `memcpy`, `_Unwind_*`, …).\n\n**0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols in the Rust `.so`** — in particular no\nunresolved `driver`/`print_hex`, i.e. no untranslated C module is being\nreferenced. Asserted by `tests/phase_d_symbols.rs::rust_so_has_no_unresolved_project_symbols`."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1710,"cache_read_input_tokens":94674,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_